+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/KNDL> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasThicknessDescription> "The group is more than 2500 m thick and possibly up to 5000 m thick."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/LBN> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasThicknessDescription> "The formation is estimated to be between 50 and 60 m thick (Holland et al., 1963; Lawson and White, 1989)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/LEL> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasThicknessDescription> "Approx. 30 to 45 m."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/LLT> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasThicknessDescription> "Estimated to be about 30 m thick."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/MELB> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasThicknessDescription> "Estimated to be between 45 and 105 m thick."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/NSF> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasThicknessDescription> "0 m to at least 20 m based on borehole records. The thickest development is located immediately east of the Culham Campus site (formerly UKAEA)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/UBB> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasThicknessDescription> "The formation is estimated to be between 12 and 45 m thick."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/UELB> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasThicknessDescription> "Estimated thickness of between 45 and 75 m (Holland et al., 1963; Lawson and White, 1989)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/ULT> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasThicknessDescription> "Estimated to be between 1.5 and 5 m thick (Holland et al., 1963; Lawson and White, 1989)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/KNDL> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasUpperBoundaryDefinition> "The upper boundary is an erosion surface, which is unconformably overlain by Upper Old Red Sandstone Group (ORSU) red-bed rocks or by basal Carboniferous strata."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/LBN> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasUpperBoundaryDefinition> "An increase in the proportion of harder limestone to calcareous siltstone distinguishes the overlying Upper Bringewood Formation (UBB) from the Lower Bringewood Formation."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/LEL> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasUpperBoundaryDefinition> "The contact with the overlying Middle Elton Formation (MELB) is transitional over a few centimetres."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/LLT> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasUpperBoundaryDefinition> "Overlain by the Upper Leintwardine Formation (ULT)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/MELB> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasUpperBoundaryDefinition> "The contact with the overlying Upper Elton Formation (UELB) is transitional."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/NSF> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasUpperBoundaryDefinition> "Unconformably overlain by the Gault Formation (GLT)."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/UBB> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasUpperBoundaryDefinition> "The base of the overlying Lower Leintwardine Formation (LLT) is defined on faunal rather than lithological criteria, i.e. above the highest occurrences of Kirkidium knightii (J. Sowerby), Strophonella euglypha (Hisinger) and tabulate corals, and therefore at the faunal change from the Mesopholidostrophia laevigata Association to the Sphaerirhynchia wilsoni Association."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/UELB> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasUpperBoundaryDefinition> "The overlying Lower Bringewood Formation (LBN) is distinguished from the Upper Elton Formation by its more irregular bedding and more homogeneous lithology."@en .
+<http://data.bgs.ac.uk/id/Lexicon/NamedRockUnit/ULT> <http://data.bgs.ac.uk/ref/Lexicon/hasUpperBoundaryDefinition> "Overlain by the Whitcliffe Formation (WHIT)."@en .
